Using the Maximum Power Transfer Theorem, \(R_L=R_{TH}\) Since a dependent source is present, apply a test voltage source \(V_{dc}\) across terminals \(a-b\) and let the current entering the network be \(I_{dc}\). Deactivate the independent source. The modified circuit is shown below. <img src="img/1ce215c40624ec5a.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Applying KCL at node \(a\), \[-I_{dc}-0.5I_1+I_1+\frac{V_{dc}}{20}=0\] where, \[I_1=\frac{V_{dc}}{40}\] Substituting, \[I_{dc}
=
\frac{V_{dc}}{80}
+
\frac{V_{dc}}{20}
=
\frac{5V_{dc}}{80}\] Hence, \[R_{TH}
=
\frac{V_{dc}}{I_{dc}}
=
\frac{80}{5}
=
16~\Omega\] Hence, the correct answer is \[\boxed{\textbf{A. }16~\Omega}\]

a) To find the short-circuit admittance (\(Y\)) parameters of the given two-port network containing a dependent current source, we perform nodal analysis at Node 1 (\(V_{1}\)) and Node 2 (\(V_{2}\)). The standard two-port network equations for \(Y\)-parameters are defined as: \[I_{1} = Y_{11} V_{1} + Y_{12} V_{2}\] \[I_{2} = Y_{21} V_{1} + Y_{22} V_{2}\] From the circuit diagram, the controlling current \(I_{x}\) flowing through the \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from Node 1 to Node 2 is given by: \[I_{x} = \frac{V_{1} - V_{2}}{5}\] Step 1: Nodal Analysis at Node 1 Applying Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL) at Node 1: \[-I_{1} + I_{x} + \frac{V_{1} - V_{2}}{10} = 0\] \[I_{1} = I_{x} + \frac{V_{1} - V_{2}}{10}\] Substituting the expression for \(I_{x}\): \[I_{1} = \frac{V_{1} - V_{2}}{5} + \frac{V_{1} - V_{2}}{10}\] \[I_{1} = (V_{1} - V_{2}) \left( \frac{1}{5} + \frac{1}{10} \right)\] \[I_{1} = 0.3 V_{1} - 0.3 V_{2} \quad \text{--- (1)}\] Comparing equation (1) with the standard admittance equation for \(I_{1}\): \(Y_{11} = \boxed{0.3\,\text{S}}\) \(Y_{12} = \boxed{-0.3\,\text{S}}\) Step 2: Nodal Analysis at Node 2 Applying Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL) at Node 2: \[-I_{2} + 2 I_{x} + \frac{V_{2}}{10} + \frac{V_{2} - V_{1}}{10} - I_{x} = 0\] \[I_{2} = I_{x} + \frac{V_{2}}{10} + \frac{V_{2} - V_{1}}{10}\] Substituting the expression for \(I_{x}\): \[I_{2} = \left(\frac{V_{1} - V_{2}}{5}\right) + \frac{V_{2}}{10} + \frac{V_{2} - V_{1}}{10}\] Grouping the coefficients by \(V_{1}\) and \(V_{2}\): \[I_{2} = V_{1} \left( \frac{1}{5} - \frac{1}{10} \right) + V_{2} \left( \frac{1}{10} + \frac{1}{10} - \frac{1}{5} \right)\] \[I_{2} = 0.1 V_{1} + 0 V_{2} \quad \text{--- (2)}\] Comparing equation (2) with the standard admittance equation for \(I_{2}\): \(Y_{21} = \boxed{0.1\,\text{S}}\) \(Y_{22} = \boxed{0\,\text{S}}\) The Y parameter matrix is : \[\begin{bmatrix}
    0.3&amp;-0.3\\0.1&amp;0
\end{bmatrix}\] b) This can be done using the following methods <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1</div>To find the open-circuit output driving-point impedance (\(Z_{22}\)) we put \(I_{1} = 0\) as \[Z_{22} = \left.\frac{V_{2}}{I_{2}}\right|_{I_{1}=0} \quad \text{--- (1)}\] The standard system describing the network using short-circuit admittance (\(Y\)) parameters is: \[I_{1} = y_{11} V_{1} + y_{12} V_{2}\] \[I_{2} = y_{21} V_{1} + y_{22} V_{2}\] Substituting the Port 1 open-circuit condition (\(I_{1} = 0\)) into the first admittance equation yields: \[0 = y_{11} V_{1} + y_{12} V_{2} \implies V_{1} = -\frac{y_{12}}{y_{11}} V_{2} \quad \text{--- (2)}\] Substituting equation (2) into the second admittance expression to isolate the terminal variable \(I_{2}\): \[I_{2} = y_{21} \left[ -\frac{y_{12} V_{2}}{y_{11}} \right] + y_{22} V_{2}\] Factoring out the shared voltage term \(V_{2}\) and establishing a common denominator: \[I_{2} = \left[ \frac{y_{11} y_{22} - y_{12} y_{21}}{y_{11}} \right] V_{2} \quad \text{--- (3)}\] The numerator of equation (3) represents the matrix determinant of the admittance parameters (\(\Delta Y\)): \[\Delta Y = y_{11} y_{22} - y_{12} y_{21}\] Rearranging equation (3) to calculate the ratio required by equation (1) provides the structural conversion formula: \[Z_{22} = \frac{V_{2}}{I_{2}} = \frac{y_{11}}{y_{11} y_{22} - y_{12} y_{21}} = \frac{y_{11}}{\Delta Y}\] Using the specific circuit admittance parameter values given in the reference solution notes (\(y_{11} = 0.3\,\text{S}\) and \(\Delta Y = 0.03\,\text{S}^2\)): \[Z_{22} = \frac{0.3}{0.03} = \boxed{10\,\Omega}\]</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2</div>We can find the \(Z\)-parameter \(Z_{22}\) from the inverse of the \(Y\)-parameter matrix. \[[Z] = [Y]^{-1}\] Now, the determinant of the admittance matrix \(|Y|\) is calculated as: \[|Y| = 0.3 \times 0 - (-0.3) \times (0.1)\] \[|Y| = 0.03\] The adjoint of the admittance matrix \(\text{adj}(Y)\) is given by: \[(\text{adj}\,Y) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 &amp; 0.3 \\ -0.1 &amp; 0.3 \end{bmatrix}\] Evaluating the inverse matrix relation: \[Z = Y^{-1} = \frac{(\text{adj}\,Y)}{|Y|} = \frac{1}{0.03} \begin{bmatrix} 0 &amp; 0.3 \\ -0.1 &amp; 0.3 \end{bmatrix}\] From the resulting matrix, the driving-point parameter \(Z_{22}\) is extracted as: \[Z_{22} = \frac{0.3}{0.03} = 10\,\Omega\]</div> <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>We can use other methods like shorting each port one by one to calculate the admittance parameter matrix.</li></ul></div> \[\boxed{[Y]=\begin{bmatrix}
    0.3&amp;-0.3\\0.1&amp;0
\end{bmatrix},Z_{22}=10\,\Omega}\]

Given frequency response is, <img src="img/86868878e54736c6.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> For \(n\) identical cascaded first-order stages, \[f_{L,\text{overall}}
=f_L\sqrt{\frac{1}{2^{1/n}-1}},\] \[f_{H,\text{overall}}
=f_H\sqrt{2^{1/n}-1}\] For \(n=3\), \[f_{L,\text{overall}}
=20\sqrt{\frac{1}{2^{1/3}-1}}
\approx39.2\,\text{Hz},\] \[f_{H,\text{overall}}
=1000\sqrt{2^{1/3}-1}
\approx509\,\text{Hz}\] which rounds to \[40\,\text{Hz}
\quad\text{and}\quad
0.5\,\text{kHz}\] Hence Option(A) is correct.

Given, \[V_{i}=2\,\text{V}, \qquad \beta=99, \qquad V_{BE}=0.7\,\text{V}\] \[\therefore V_{E}=V_{i}=2\,\text{V}\] The emitter current is \[I_{E}=I_0=\frac{V_{E}-(-15)}{10}
      =\frac{2+15}{10}
      =1.7\,\text{A}\] Since \[I_{E}=I_{C}+I_{B}=(\beta+1)I_{B},\] \[I_{B}=\frac{I_{E}}{\beta+1}
     =\frac{1.7}{100}
     =17\,\text{mA}\] The base voltage is \[V_{B}=V_{E}+V_{BE}
     =2+0.7
     =2.7\,\text{V}\] The base current flows through the resistor \(R_{B}=150\,\text{k}\Omega\), hence \[I_{B}=\frac{V_{op}-V_{B}}{R_{B}}\] \[V_{op}=V_{B}+I_{B}R_{B}\] \[V_{op}
=2.7+(17\times10^{-3})(150\times10^{3})
=2.7+2550
=2552.7\,\text{V}\] This value is much larger than the op-amp supply voltage of \(\pm12\,\text{V}\). Hence, an ideal op-amp supplied by \(\pm12\,\text{V}\) cannot produce this output and will saturate at its positive supply. Therefore, \[\boxed{I_{o}=1.7\,\text{A}}\] \[\boxed{V_{op}=+12\,\text{V}\;(\text{positive saturation})}\]

The DC equivalent circuit is, <img src="img/192f763a2ed9ecfe.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Given \(\beta=100, V_{BE}=0.7\,\text{V} \text{ and } 
V_{Z}=10.7\,\text{V}\) Since the Zener diode is in breakdown and has zero dynamic resistance, \[V_{B2}=V_{EE}+V_{Z}
=-10+10.7
=0.7\,\text{V}\] Hence, the emitter voltage of transistor \(Q_{2}\) is \[V_{E2}=V_{B2}-V_{BE}
=0.7-0.7
=0\,\text{V}\] The emitter of \(Q_{2}\) is directly connected to the collector of \(Q_{1}\). \[\therefore V_{C1}=V_{E2}=0\,\text{V}\] The base voltage of \(Q_1\) is, \[V_{B1}=V_{E1}+V_{BE1}\] \[V_{B1}=V_{EE}+0.7
=-10+0.7
=-9.3\,\text{V}.\] The current through the base resistor is \[I_{RB}=I_{B1}
=\frac{0-(-9.3)}{930\,\text{k}\Omega}
=\frac{9.3}{930\times10^{3}}
=10\,\mu\text{A}.\] \[I_{C1}
=\beta I_{B1}
=100\times10\,\mu\text{A}
=1\,\text{mA}.\] \[\boxed{I_{C1}=1\,\text{mA}\quad V_{C1}=0\,\text{V}}.\]

Given \(\beta=100,  r_{o}=\infty\text{ and }
V_{T}=25\,\text{mV}\). From above question, \[I_{C1}=1\,\text{mA}\] Since the transistors are identical and connected in series, \[I_{C2}=I_{C1}=1\,\text{mA}\] Hence, the transconductance of each transistor is \[g_{m}=\frac{I_{C}}{V_{T}}
=\frac{1\,\text{mA}}{25\,\text{mV}}
=0.04\,\text{S}\] \[r_{\pi}=\frac{\beta}{g_{m}}
=\frac{100}{0.04}
=2.5\,\text{k}\Omega\] <strong>Voltage gain of the first stage (CE):</strong> The resistance seen looking into the emitter of \(Q_2\) is \(1/g_m\) which acts as collector resistance for \(Q_1\). So the AC equivalent of the common emitter stage is redrawn as, <img src="img/a9980ec8d7c976a8.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> \[R_{C1}\approx\frac{1}{g_{m}}
=\frac{1}{0.04}
=25\,\Omega\] The voltage gain of common emitter stage without emitter resistor is, \[A_{v1}=\frac{V_{C_1}}{V_S}
=-g_{m}R_{C1}
=-0.04\times25
=-1\] <strong>Voltage gain of the second stage (CB):</strong> The AC equivalent circuit of common base stage is given as, <img src="img/397b4a7677ff34b7.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> The gain of a common-base stage is \[A_{v2}=\frac{V_0}{V_{C_1}}
=g_{m}(R_{C2}\parallel R_{L})
=0.04\times2000
=80\] Hence, the overall voltage gain is \[A_{v}=A_{v1}\times A_{v2}=\frac{V_{C_1}}{V_S}\times\frac{V_0}{V_{C_1}}=\frac{V_0}{V_S}
=(-1)(80)
=-80.\] \[\boxed{A_{v}=\frac{V_{o}}{V_{s}}=-80}\]

Let the transfer function of the all-pole second-order system be \[G(s)=\frac{K}{s^{2}+as+b}\] Substituting \(s=j\omega\), \[G(j\omega)=\frac{K}{(b-\omega^{2})+ja\omega}\] From the Nyquist plot, \[G(j0)=2\] Therefore, \[\frac{K}{b}=2\] \[K=2b\] Also, the plot passes through the point \((0,-2)\) at \(\omega=2\,\text{rad/sec}\). Hence, \[G(j2)=-j2\] Substituting \(\omega=2\), \[G(j2)=\frac{K}{(b-4)+j2a}\] Rationalizing, \[G(j2)
=
\frac{K[(b-4)-j2a]}
{(b-4)^{2}+4a^{2}}
=
\frac{K(b-4)}
{(b-4)^{2}+4a^{2}}
-
j\frac{2aK}
{(b-4)^{2}+4a^{2}}\] Since the Nyquist plot lies on the imaginary axis at \(\omega=2\), the real part must be zero. \[\frac{K(b-4)}
{(b-4)^{2}+4a^{2}}=0\] \[b-4=0\] \[b=4\] From the earlier result, \[K=2b\] \[K=2(4)=8\] Therefore, \[G(j2)=\frac{8}{j2a}\] Rationalizing, \[G(j2)=\frac{8}{j2a}\times\frac{-j}{-j}
=\frac{-8j}{2a}
=-\frac{4j}{a}\] But from the Nyquist plot, \[G(j2)=-j2\] Comparing imaginary parts, \[\frac{4}{a}=2\] \[a=2\] Hence, the transfer function is \[\boxed{G(s)=\frac{8}{s^{2}+2s+4}}\]

From the given block diagram, \(x_1(t)\) and \(x_2(t)\) are chosen as the state variables. <img src="img/7efc5e2c7674691a.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,420px)"> From the block diagram, the input state equations are: \[\dot{x}_1(t)=-x_1(t)+x_2(t)+u(t)\] \[\dot{x}_2(t)=-2x_2(t)-u(t)\] The output state equation is, \[y(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)\] Arranging the equations in matrix form, \[\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x}_1(t)\\
\dot{x}_2(t)
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
-1 &amp; 1\\
0 &amp; -2
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1(t)\\
x_2(t)
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
-1
\end{bmatrix}
u(t)\]

From the state model, \[\dot{x}(t)=Ax(t)+Bu(t)\] Comparing with the equations obtained in Q9.02.2, \[A=
\begin{bmatrix}
-1 &amp; 1\\
0 &amp; -2
\end{bmatrix}\] State transition matrix is given by, \[\phi(t)=\mathcal{L}^{-1}\left\{(sI-A)^{-1}\right\}\] First compute, \[sI-A
=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s+1 &amp; -1\\
0 &amp; s+2
\end{array}\right]\] Determinant is, \[|sI-A|
=
(s+1)(s+2)\] Therefore, \[(sI-A)^{-1}
=
\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s+2 &amp; 1\\
0 &amp; s+1
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\dfrac{1}{s+1} &amp;
\dfrac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}
\\ 0 &amp;
\dfrac{1}{s+2}
\end{array}\right]\] Using partial fractions, \[\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}
=
\frac{1}{s+1}
-
\frac{1}{s+2}\] Taking inverse Laplace transform element wise, \[\phi(t)
=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
e^{-t} &amp;
e^{-t}-e^{-2t}
\\ 0 &amp;
e^{-2t}
\end{array}\right]\]

Given initial conditions are, \[x_1(0)=1,
\qquad
x_2(0)=1\] Therefore, \[x(0)
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
1
\end{bmatrix}\] Assuming zero input, \[u(t)=0\] Hence, the state equation becomes \[\dot{x}(t)=Ax(t)\] The solution of the homogeneous state equation is, \[x(t)=\phi(t)x(0)\] From Q9.02.3, \[\phi(t)
=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
e^{-t} &amp;
e^{-t}-e^{-2t}
\\ 0 &amp;
e^{-2t}
\end{array}\right]\] Substituting the initial condition vector, \[x(t)
=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
e^{-t} &amp;
e^{-t}-e^{-2t}
\\ 0 &amp;
e^{-2t}
\end{array}\right]
\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
1
\end{array}\right]\] \[x(t)
=
\left[\begin{array}{c}
e^{-t}(1)+\left(e^{-t}-e^{-2t}\right)(1)
\\ 0(1)+e^{-2t}(1)
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\left[\begin{array}{c}
e^{-t}+e^{-t}-e^{-2t}
\\ e^{-2t}
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\left[\begin{array}{c}
2e^{-t}-e^{-2t}
\\ e^{-2t}
\end{array}\right]\] Therefore, \[x_1(t)=2e^{-t}-e^{-2t}\] \[x_2(t)=e^{-2t}\] From the output equation, \[y(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)\] Substituting the values of the state variables, \[y(t)
=
\left(2e^{-t}-e^{-2t}\right)
+
e^{-2t}\] \[y(t)=2e^{-t}\] \[\boxed{
y(t)=2e^{-t}
}\]

The controller is \[G_{c}(s)=\frac{k}{s}\] The open-loop transfer function of the compensated system is \[G(s)=G_{c}(s)G_{p}(s)
=
\frac{k}{s(s+1)(2s+1)}\] Substituting \(s=j\omega\), \[G(j\omega)=\frac{k}{j\omega(1+j\omega)(1+2j\omega)}\] The phase angle is \[\angle G(j\omega)
=
-90^{\circ}
-\tan^{-1}(\omega)
-\tan^{-1}(2\omega)\] At phase crossover frequency, \[\angle G(j\omega_{pc})=-180^{\circ}\] Hence, \[-90^{\circ}-\tan^{-1}(\omega_{pc})
-
\tan^{-1}(2\omega_{pc})
=
180^{\circ}\] \[\tan^{-1}(\omega_{pc})
+
\tan^{-1}(2\omega_{pc})
=
90^{\circ}\] Using the identity, \[\tan^{-1}(A)+\tan^{-1}(B)=90^{\circ}
\quad \Rightarrow \quad AB=1\] Therefore, \[\omega_{pc}(2\omega_{pc})=1\] \[2\omega_{pc}^{2}=1\] \[\omega_{pc}^{2}=\frac{1}{2}\] \[\omega_{pc}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\] Now, \[|G(j\omega)|
=
\frac{k}
{\omega\sqrt{1+\omega^{2}}\sqrt{1+4\omega^{2}}}\] Substituting \[\omega=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\] \[|G(j\omega_{pc})|
=
\dfrac{k}
{
\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)
\sqrt{1+\dfrac{1}{2}}
\sqrt{1+2}
}
=
\dfrac{k}
{
\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)
\sqrt{\dfrac{3}{2}}
\sqrt{3}
}
=
\frac{k}{3/2}\] \[|G(j\omega_{pc})|
=
\frac{2k}{3}\] Gain margin is \[GM=\frac{1}{|G(j\omega_{pc})|}\] Given, \[GM=2.5\] Therefore, \[2.5=\frac{1}{2k/3}\] \[k=\frac{3}{5}=0.6\] \[\boxed{k=0.6}\]

The compensated open-loop transfer function is \[G(s)=\frac{0.6}{s(s+1)(2s+1)}\] Since there is one pole at origin, the system is a Type-\(1\) system. Steady state error for unit-step input For a Type-\(1\) system, \[e_{ss,\text{step}}=0\] \[\boxed{e_{ss,\text{step}}=0}\] Steady state error for unit-ramp input Velocity error constant, \[K_{v}
=
\lim_{s\to0}sG(s)\] \[K_{v}
=
\lim_{s\to0}
\frac{0.6}{(s+1)(2s+1)}\] \[K_{v}=0.6\] Hence, \[e_{ss,\text{ramp}}
=
\frac{1}{K_{v}}
=
\frac{1}{0.6}
=
\frac{5}{3}\] \[\boxed{e_{ss,\text{ramp}}=\frac{5}{3}}\]
